# Example events/*.json bridge target — see events/example_pvp_damage_multiplier.json.
#
# Demonstrates EntityDamageByEntityWrapper — everything EntityDamageEvent has (event.entity,
# event.damage/set_damage, event.cause) plus event.damager, the entity that CAUSED the damage. With
# the generic BukkitEvent wrapper, telling "a player hit something" from "a skeleton's arrow hit
# something" apart means reflectively calling getDamager() yourself and hoping the returned object
# happens to expose the methods you then guess at — here damager comes back already wrapped as
# whatever PolyType actually fits it ("Player" for a real player, a generic entity object
# otherwise), so type_of(event.damager) is a real, always-correct answer instead of a coercion
# gamble.
#
# Also shows event.cause — "fall" damage never reaches this wrapper at all (there's no damager to
# report), so this script only ever sees genuinely entity-caused hits.
def on_damage():
    if type_of(event.damager) == "Player" && type_of(event.entity) == "Player":
        # Player-vs-player: double it. Skips PvE entirely (a player vs. a zombie is a Player
        # damager but a non-Player victim) so this only ever touches real PvP.
        event.set_damage(event.damage * 2.0)
    end
end
